Installing greyd
================

Requirements
------------

  * Go 1.25 or later (https://go.dev/dl/); go.mod pins the patched 1.25.x
    toolchain, which the go command downloads automatically (set
    GOTOOLCHAIN=local to build with the installed release instead)
  * GNU make
  * curl at run time, used by greyd-setup to fetch blacklists over HTTP/FTP
  * Java, only if you edit the configuration grammar and need to run
    "make generate" (see below); the generated parser sources are committed

All database drivers (sqlite, mysql, postgresql, bolt, memory), all firewall
drivers (netfilter, pf, dummy) and SPF support are compiled into the programs.
There are no build-time feature switches and no shared objects to install.

Building
--------

    $ make

builds greyd, greydb, greyd-setup and greylogd into bin/. The Go toolchain
downloads the module dependencies on the first build.

Testing
-------

    $ make test

runs go vet and the unit tests with the race detector. The MySQL and
PostgreSQL driver tests need a database server; to run them against
throw-away containers use:

    $ make test-db-docker

Installing
----------

    $ sudo make install

installs the programs into $(sbindir), the sample configuration and init
scripts into $(sysconfdir)/greyd and the manual pages into $(mandir). The
usual GNU variables are honoured, on the command line or in the environment:

    prefix          default /usr/local
    exec_prefix     default $(prefix)
    sbindir         default $(exec_prefix)/sbin
    sysconfdir      default $(prefix)/etc
    localstatedir   default $(prefix)/var
    mandir          default $(prefix)/share/man
    DESTDIR         staging directory for packaging

The following paths are compiled into the programs and substituted into
the sample configuration files. Pass the same values to "make" and to
"make install":

    DEFAULT_CONFIG    default $(sysconfdir)/greyd/greyd.conf
    GREYD_PIDFILE     default $(localstatedir)/empty/greyd/greyd.pid
    GREYLOGD_PIDFILE  default $(localstatedir)/empty/greylogd/greylogd.pid

For example, for a system-wide installation matching the RPM and Debian
packages:

    $ make prefix=/usr sysconfdir=/etc localstatedir=/var \
           DEFAULT_CONFIG=/etc/greyd/greyd.conf \
           GREYD_PIDFILE=/var/empty/greyd/greyd.pid \
           GREYLOGD_PIDFILE=/var/empty/greylogd/greylogd.pid
    $ sudo make install prefix=/usr sysconfdir=/etc localstatedir=/var \
           DEFAULT_CONFIG=/etc/greyd/greyd.conf \
           GREYD_PIDFILE=/var/empty/greyd/greyd.pid \
           GREYLOGD_PIDFILE=/var/empty/greylogd/greylogd.pid

"make uninstall" removes the installed programs and manual pages.

Users and directories
---------------------

greyd runs as two unprivileged users: the main connection handling process
runs as "greyd" inside a chroot, and the greylisting processes (which hold
the database handle) run as "greydb". Create them along with the chroot and
database directories (the paths below are those used by the packages; adjust
them if you changed prefix or localstatedir):

    # groupadd greyd
    # useradd -M -g greyd -d /var/empty/greyd -s /bin/false greyd
    # groupadd greydb
    # useradd -M -g greydb -d /var/lib/greyd -s /bin/false greydb

    # install -d -m 0750 -o greyd  -g greyd  /var/empty/greyd
    # install -d -m 0750 -o greydb -g greydb /var/empty/greylogd
    # install -d -m 0750 -o greydb -g greydb /var/lib/greyd

/var/empty/greyd is the chroot_dir of the main greyd process and must contain
the greyd pidfile (GREYD_PIDFILE above). /var/empty/greylogd holds the
greylogd pidfile. The database directory is the "path" option of the
database section in greyd.conf; the sample configuration sets it to
$(localstatedir)/greyd and the bolt and sqlite drivers default to
/var/db/greyd when it is not set. It must be writable by the "greydb" user.

Configuration
-------------

Copy or edit $(sysconfdir)/greyd/greyd.conf; see greyd.conf(5) for every
option. Select the drivers by name:

    section firewall {
        driver = "netfilter"    # or "pf", "dummy"
        ...
    }

    section database {
        driver = "bolt"         # or "sqlite", "mysql", "postgresql", "memory"
        path = "/var/lib/greyd"
        ...
    }

Configuration files from previous releases that reference driver shared
objects (eg driver = "/usr/lib/greyd/greyd_sqlite.so") are still accepted;
the former Berkeley DB drivers map to bolt.

The netfilter driver needs the CAP_NET_ADMIN capability, which greyd retains
when dropping privileges. Example iptables rules for redirecting SMTP
connections to greyd and for blacklist-only mode are given in greyd(8).

Blacklists are fetched and loaded into greyd by greyd-setup(8), which should
be run periodically from cron, for example at 5 minutes past every hour:

    05 * * * * /usr/sbin/greyd-setup -f /etc/greyd/greyd.conf

Init scripts for RHEL-like systems and Debian are installed alongside the
sample configuration as greyd.redhat-init, greylogd.redhat-init,
greyd.debian-init and greylogd.debian-init.

systemd
-------

"make install" also installs systemd units, generated from the templates
in packages/systemd/ with the configured paths, into $(unitdir), which
defaults to $(prefix)/lib/systemd/system (pass unitdir=/lib/systemd/system
or unitdir=/etc/systemd/system if your installation prefix is not on
systemd's search path):

    greyd.service         the main daemon (greyd -F, supervised in the
                          foreground)
    greylogd.service      the whitelist logger (Type=forking, follows
                          greylogd_pidfile)
    greyd-setup.service   one-shot blacklist refresh
    greyd-setup.timer     runs greyd-setup.service daily
    greyd-monitor.service the Prometheus exporter (runs as the greyd user;
                          needs config_socket in greyd.conf)
    greyd.socket, greyd-config.socket, greyd-unprivileged.service
                          socket activated alternative to greyd.service
                          that never runs as root (see below)

The daemons are started as root and drop privileges themselves (see the
drop_privs option in greyd.conf(5)); the units confine them with a reduced
capability set and a read-only filesystem view apart from the state,
runtime, pidfile and log directories. After creating the users and
directories described above:

    # systemctl daemon-reload
    # systemctl enable --now greyd
    # systemctl enable --now greylogd
    # systemctl enable --now greyd-setup.timer
    # systemctl start greyd-setup       # load the blacklists straight away

The timer replaces the cron job above; override OnCalendar= in
/etc/systemd/system/greyd-setup.timer.d/ to run it more often.

Running greyd without root (Linux): systemd can bind the SMTP port and the
configuration socket and start greyd as the "greyd" user with only
CAP_NET_ADMIN (for ipset and conntrack). greyd adopts the passed sockets,
skips the chroot (Landlock confines the filesystem instead) and keeps the
service user for all three processes, so the database directory must be
owned by "greyd" rather than "greydb" in this mode:

    # chown -R greyd:greyd /var/lib/greyd
    # systemctl disable --now greyd
    # systemctl enable --now greyd.socket greyd-config.socket

greyd-setup and greyd-monitor talk to /run/greyd/config.sock in this setup;
set config_socket = "/run/greyd/config.sock" in greyd.conf so that they find
it. See SOCKET ACTIVATION in greyd(8).

Monitoring: greyd-monitor(8) serves Prometheus metrics on 127.0.0.1:9143
by default (section "monitor" in greyd.conf(5)); "greyd --stats" prints the
same counters and "greydb -s" summarises the database.

Packaging and releases
----------------------

    $ make packages

builds deb and rpm packages into dist/ with nfpm (https://nfpm.goreleaser.com;
"go install github.com/goreleaser/nfpm/v2/cmd/nfpm@latest" if the target
reports it missing). The programs are rebuilt with the package paths
(prefix=/usr, sysconfdir=/etc, the database under /var/lib/greyd and the
pidfiles under /var/empty, see PKG_MAKEVARS in packages/release.mk) into
packages/nfpm/stage/ together with the generated configuration, units and
manual pages, and packages/nfpm/nfpm.yaml describes the package. The
post-install script creates the greyd and greydb users and the directories
listed above; greyd.conf is a conffile (config|noreplace) so local edits
survive upgrades. The units are installed but not enabled.

Tagged releases (v*) are built by .github/workflows/release.yml with
goreleaser from .goreleaser.yaml: static builds of every program for
linux/amd64, linux/arm64, freebsd/amd64 and openbsd/amd64, reproducible
tar.gz archives, the same deb and rpm packages, checksums.txt, a syft SBOM
(*.sbom.json) for each archive and package, and checksums.txt.sig with
checksums.txt.pem, a keyless cosign signature made with the workflow's
OIDC identity. Verify a download with:

    $ cosign verify-blob --certificate checksums.txt.pem \
          --signature checksums.txt.sig \
          --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/mikey-austin/greyd-golang/' \
          --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
          checksums.txt
    $ sha256sum -c --ignore-missing checksums.txt

"make release-check" validates the goreleaser configuration and
"make release-snapshot" builds everything into dist/ without publishing.

Regenerating the configuration parser
-------------------------------------

The greyd.conf parser is generated with ANTLR4 from
internal/config/grammar/GreydConf.g4. The generated Go sources are committed
so a normal build does not need Java. After editing the grammar run:

    $ make generate

which downloads the pinned ANTLR tool jar into .tools/ (using curl) and
regenerates the lexer and parser.
